course: dictionary
lesson: 12475
title: Defraud: tricking people out of money
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: To defraud someone means to trick them out of money, property or rights by lying or cheating. It is a verb, so it describes an action: defraud, defrauds, defrauded, defrauding. Look for the clues: «The man lied about the prize and defrauded the old lady of her savings.» Lied and cheating tell you that she was tricked.
explain: Look at what was taken. Someone is defrauded of something, so the words of or out of tell you what was lost. «Thieves defrauded the shop of thousands of pounds.» Ask yourself: did someone lose money, property or rights because another person lied?
explain: Defraud is a serious word used in law. The crime is called fraud, and a person who defrauds people can be charged in court. «The two men were charged with conspiracy to defraud.» Conspiracy means planning something secretly with others, so this means they planned together to trick people.
fact: Defraud is a verb: defraud, defrauds, defrauded, defrauding.
fact: «Defraud» means to trick someone out of money, property or rights by lying or cheating.
fact: Fraud is the crime, and it is against the law.
fact: Scam emails, fake phone calls and bogus sellers try to defraud people.
fact: Never share bank details or passwords, and tell an adult about any strange message.
exercise: «The fake seller tried to defraud Sam of his money.» What does defraud mean here? | To mend something that is broken | *To trick someone out of money by lying | To run very fast | To give someone a present
exercise: «The email said he had won a prize, but it was a scam to defraud people.» Which word tells you the email was a trick? | email | prize | *scam | won
exercise: You get a strange message asking for your bank details. What should you do? | Send your bank details to the sender | *Tell an adult about it | Reply with your home address | Share your password so they can help
test: «She was defrauded of her savings by a fake website.» What was taken from her? | Her house | *Her savings | Her shoes | Her dog
test: Which word fits? «The men had ___ people of their money for years.» | defrauds | *defrauded | defrauding | defraud
exam: «The court heard how the group planned to defraud hundreds of people.» What does planning to defraud people mean? | Planning a birthday party | *Planning to trick people out of their money | Planning to give people gifts | Planning to paint a house
